/* AI brain Q&A pack 05: everyday life */
(window.AIQA = window.AIQA || []).push(
  ["how do i make pancakes | pancake recipe | easy pancakes | how to make pancakes from scratch", "Here's an easy pancake recipe:\n1. Whisk 1 cup flour, 1 tbsp sugar, 2 tsp baking powder and a pinch of salt.\n2. In another bowl mix 1 cup milk, 1 egg and 2 tbsp melted butter.\n3. Stir the wet into the dry until just combined (a few lumps are fine).\n4. Pour about 1/4 cup onto a hot greased pan; flip when bubbles form on top, then cook 1 more minute."],
  ["how do i make scrambled eggs | scrambled eggs recipe | how to scramble eggs | fluffy scrambled eggs", "For soft scrambled eggs:\n1. Crack 2–3 eggs into a bowl, add a pinch of salt and whisk well.\n2. Melt a little butter in a pan over **low-medium** heat.\n3. Pour in the eggs and gently push them around with a spatula.\n4. Take them off the heat while they still look slightly wet — they finish cooking on the plate."],
  ["how do i boil an egg | how to hard boil an egg | hard boiled eggs | how long to boil an egg", "To hard-boil eggs:\n1. Place eggs in a pot and cover with cold water by about an inch.\n2. Bring to a boil, then turn off the heat and cover the pot.\n3. Let them sit 10–12 minutes.\n4. Move them to cold water for a few minutes, then peel."],
  ["how do i make a soft boiled egg | soft boiled egg | runny egg yolk | dippy eggs", "Lower an egg gently into already-boiling water and cook for about **6 minutes** for a runny yolk (7 for jammy). Then cool it in cold water for a minute and crack the top off. Great with toast soldiers!"],
  ["how do i fry an egg | fried egg | sunny side up egg | how to cook an egg in a pan", "Heat a little butter or oil in a pan over medium heat, crack the egg in, and cook until the white is set — about 2–3 minutes for sunny side up. For over easy, gently flip it and cook 30 more seconds."],
  ["how do i boil pasta | how to cook pasta | how long to boil pasta | cooking spaghetti", "To cook pasta:\n1. Bring a big pot of water to a rolling boil and add a good pinch of salt.\n2. Add the pasta and stir so it doesn't stick.\n3. Cook for the time on the package (usually 8–12 minutes), tasting near the end.\n4. Drain in a colander, saving a splash of pasta water for your sauce."],
  ["how do i cook rice | how to make rice on the stove | rice recipe | how much water for rice", "For white rice on the stove:\n1. Rinse 1 cup rice until the water runs mostly clear.\n2. Add it to a pot with about 1.5–2 cups water and a pinch of salt.\n3. Bring to a boil, then cover and turn to low for about 15–18 minutes.\n4. Turn off the heat, keep covered 5 minutes, then fluff with a fork."],
  ["how do i make a grilled cheese | grilled cheese sandwich | how to make grilled cheese | toasted cheese sandwich", "Grilled cheese:\n1. Butter one side of two slices of bread.\n2. Put one slice butter-side down in a pan on medium-low heat and add cheese.\n3. Top with the other slice, butter-side up.\n4. Cook 2–4 minutes per side until golden and melty. Low heat is the secret!"],
  ["how do i make chocolate chip cookies | cookie recipe | easy cookies | how to bake cookies", "Basic chocolate chip cookies:\n1. Cream 1/2 cup soft butter with 1/2 cup brown sugar and 1/4 cup white sugar.\n2. Beat in 1 egg and 1 tsp vanilla.\n3. Mix in 1 1/4 cups flour, 1/2 tsp baking soda and a pinch of salt, then 1 cup chocolate chips.\n4. Scoop onto a lined tray and bake at 350°F (175°C) for 9–11 minutes. Ask an adult to help with the oven!"],
  ["how do i make a smoothie | smoothie recipe | easy smoothie | fruit smoothie", "Easy smoothie: add 1 banana, about 1 cup of frozen berries, 1/2 cup yogurt and 1/2–1 cup milk or juice to a blender. Blend until smooth, adding more liquid if it's too thick. Toss in a handful of spinach — you won't taste it!"],
  ["how do i make french toast | french toast recipe | eggy bread", "French toast:\n1. Whisk 2 eggs, 1/2 cup milk, a little cinnamon and a dash of vanilla.\n2. Dip bread slices in the mix for a few seconds per side.\n3. Cook in a buttered pan over medium heat, 2–3 minutes per side until golden.\n4. Serve with syrup, fruit or a dusting of powdered sugar."],
  ["how do i make an omelette | omelet recipe | how to make an omelet | cheese omelette", "Omelette:\n1. Whisk 2–3 eggs with a pinch of salt.\n2. Pour into a buttered nonstick pan over medium heat.\n3. As it sets, gently lift the edges so runny egg flows underneath.\n4. When almost set, add cheese or fillings to one half, fold it over and slide onto a plate."],
  ["how do i make toast without a toaster | toast in a pan | toast in the oven", "Put bread in a dry pan over medium heat and toast 1–2 minutes per side, watching closely. Or put slices on a tray under the oven broiler for 1–2 minutes per side — they burn fast, so don't walk away!"],
  ["how do i make oatmeal | oatmeal recipe | how to cook porridge | porridge recipe", "Simmer 1/2 cup rolled oats with 1 cup milk or water and a pinch of salt for about 5 minutes, stirring often, until creamy. Top with banana, berries, a drizzle of honey, cinnamon or a spoon of peanut butter."],
  ["how do i make mashed potatoes | mashed potato recipe | mash potatoes", "Mashed potatoes:\n1. Peel and cut potatoes into chunks.\n2. Cover with cold salted water, bring to a boil and cook 15–20 minutes until a fork slides in easily.\n3. Drain, then mash with butter and a splash of warm milk.\n4. Season with salt and pepper to taste."],
  ["how do i bake a potato | baked potato | jacket potato | how long to bake a potato", "Scrub a potato, poke it a few times with a fork, rub with a little oil and salt, and bake at 400°F (200°C) for about 45–60 minutes until soft inside. Top with butter, cheese, sour cream or beans."],
  ["how do i make a sandwich | sandwich ideas | good sandwich ideas", "A tasty sandwich has bread, a protein, something crunchy and a spread. Try: turkey + cheese + lettuce + mayo, peanut butter + banana, hummus + cucumber + carrot, or egg salad. Toasting the bread makes it extra good."],
  ["how do i make a peanut butter and jelly sandwich | pb&j | pbj sandwich", "Spread peanut butter on one slice of bread and jelly or jam on the other, then press them together. Tip: put a thin layer of peanut butter on both slices and the jelly in the middle so the bread doesn't get soggy."],
  ["how do i make popcorn on the stove | stovetop popcorn | how to pop popcorn", "Heat 2–3 tbsp oil in a large pot over medium-high heat, add 1/2 cup kernels and cover with a lid. Shake the pot now and then; when popping slows to a few seconds apart, take it off the heat. Season with salt or melted butter."],
  ["how do i make hot chocolate | hot cocoa recipe | how to make cocoa", "Warm 1 cup milk in a pot or microwave (don't let it boil). Stir in 1–2 tbsp cocoa powder and 1–2 tbsp sugar until smooth — or use a hot chocolate mix. Top with marshmallows or whipped cream!"],
  ["how do i make lemonade | homemade lemonade | lemonade recipe", "Homemade lemonade: stir 1 cup fresh lemon juice (about 5–6 lemons) with 3/4 cup sugar until dissolved, then add 4–5 cups cold water and lots of ice. Taste and add more water or sugar as you like."],
  ["how do i make a salad | easy salad | simple salad recipe", "Start with greens (lettuce, spinach), add color (tomato, cucumber, carrot, peppers), something filling (beans, chicken, cheese, egg) and something crunchy (nuts, croutons). Toss with a simple dressing of 3 parts oil to 1 part vinegar or lemon, plus salt and pepper."],
  ["how do i make salad dressing | vinaigrette recipe | homemade dressing", "Simple vinaigrette: shake 3 tbsp olive oil, 1 tbsp vinegar or lemon juice, 1/2 tsp mustard, a pinch of salt and pepper, and a little honey in a jar with a lid. Shake well before using."],
  ["how do i make pizza at home | homemade pizza | easy pizza recipe", "Quick homemade pizza:\n1. Roll out store-bought dough (or use a flatbread or naan).\n2. Spread on tomato sauce and sprinkle mozzarella.\n3. Add toppings you like.\n4. Bake at around 450°F (230°C) for 10–15 minutes until the crust is golden and the cheese bubbles."],
  ["how do i make a quesadilla | quesadilla recipe | cheese quesadilla", "Put a tortilla in a pan over medium heat, sprinkle cheese on half (add beans, chicken or peppers if you like), fold it over and cook 2–3 minutes per side until crispy and melted. Cut into wedges and serve with salsa."],
  ["how do i make tacos | taco night | easy tacos recipe", "Easy tacos: brown ground meat (or beans) in a pan, drain any extra fat, and add taco seasoning with a splash of water. Spoon into warm shells or tortillas and top with lettuce, cheese, tomato, salsa and sour cream."],
  ["how do i make soup | easy soup recipe | how to make vegetable soup", "Simple vegetable soup:\n1. Cook chopped onion, carrot and celery in a little oil for 5–8 minutes.\n2. Add garlic, then any other chopped veggies.\n3. Pour in broth to cover, plus canned tomatoes if you like.\n4. Simmer 20–30 minutes until tender, then season with salt and pepper."],
  ["how do i make a cake | easy cake recipe | simple vanilla cake | how to bake a cake", "Simple vanilla cake:\n1. Beat 1/2 cup butter with 1 cup sugar, then add 2 eggs and 2 tsp vanilla.\n2. Mix in 1 1/2 cups flour and 1 3/4 tsp baking powder, alternating with 1/2 cup milk.\n3. Pour into a greased 8-inch pan.\n4. Bake at 350°F (175°C) for 30–35 minutes, until a toothpick comes out clean."],
  ["how do i make brownies | brownie recipe | easy brownies", "Easy brownies: melt 1/2 cup butter, stir in 1 cup sugar, 2 eggs and 1 tsp vanilla, then add 1/3 cup cocoa, 1/2 cup flour and a pinch of salt. Spread in a greased 8-inch pan and bake at 350°F (175°C) for 20–25 minutes. Don't overbake — fudgy is good!"],
  ["how do i make muffins | muffin recipe | blueberry muffins", "Blueberry muffins:\n1. Mix 2 cups flour, 3/4 cup sugar, 2 tsp baking powder and 1/2 tsp salt.\n2. In another bowl mix 1 egg, 1 cup milk and 1/3 cup oil.\n3. Stir together just until combined, then fold in 1 cup blueberries.\n4. Fill a lined muffin tin and bake at 375°F (190°C) for 20–25 minutes."],
  ["how do i make banana bread | banana bread recipe | what to do with overripe bananas", "Banana bread is perfect for spotty bananas! Mash 3 ripe bananas, stir in 1/3 cup melted butter, 3/4 cup sugar, 1 egg and 1 tsp vanilla. Add 1 tsp baking soda, a pinch of salt and 1 1/2 cups flour. Bake in a greased loaf pan at 350°F (175°C) for about 55–60 minutes."],
  ["how do i make waffles | waffle recipe | homemade waffles", "Mix 2 cups flour, 1 tbsp sugar, 1 tbsp baking powder and 1/2 tsp salt. Whisk in 2 eggs, 1 3/4 cups milk and 1/2 cup melted butter. Pour into a preheated, greased waffle iron and cook until golden and it stops steaming."],
  ["how do i make mac and cheese | macaroni and cheese recipe | homemade mac and cheese", "Stovetop mac and cheese:\n1. Cook 2 cups macaroni and drain.\n2. In the same pot melt 2 tbsp butter, stir in 2 tbsp flour, then whisk in 1 1/2 cups milk until it thickens.\n3. Stir in 1 1/2–2 cups shredded cheddar until melted.\n4. Add the pasta back, season with salt and pepper, and enjoy."],
  ["how do i cook chicken breast | how to cook chicken | how do i know chicken is done", "Pan-cook a chicken breast over medium heat in a little oil about 6–7 minutes per side, or bake at 400°F (200°C) for 20–25 minutes. It's done when the inside is no longer pink and a food thermometer reads **165°F (74°C)**. Always wash your hands after touching raw chicken."],
  ["how do i cook bacon | crispy bacon | bacon in the oven", "Lay bacon strips on a foil-lined tray and bake at 400°F (200°C) for 15–20 minutes until crispy — less splatter than a pan. Drain on paper towels. Careful: the grease is very hot."],
  ["how do i make hamburgers | burger recipe | how to cook burgers", "Gently shape ground beef into patties a little wider than your bun, press a small dent in the middle and season with salt and pepper. Cook in a hot pan or grill about 4–5 minutes per side. Ground beef should reach 160°F (71°C) inside."],
  ["how do i steam vegetables | steamed broccoli | how to cook vegetables", "Put about an inch of water in a pot, bring it to a boil, place veggies in a steamer basket above the water and cover. Broccoli takes about 5 minutes, carrots 6–8. They're done when bright and just tender."],
  ["how do i roast vegetables | roasted veggies | oven roasted vegetables", "Cut veggies into similar-size pieces, toss with olive oil, salt and pepper, and spread on a tray in one layer. Roast at 425°F (220°C) for 20–35 minutes, flipping halfway, until browned at the edges."],
  ["how do i make guacamole | guacamole recipe | avocado dip", "Mash 2 ripe avocados with the juice of half a lime and a pinch of salt. Stir in a little chopped onion, tomato and cilantro if you like. Serve right away with chips."],
  ["how do i make a parfait | yogurt parfait | healthy breakfast idea", "Layer yogurt, fresh fruit (berries or banana) and granola in a glass or jar, then repeat. Add a drizzle of honey on top. It's quick, pretty and healthy!"],
  ["how do i make trail mix | homemade trail mix | snack mix", "Mix equal-ish amounts of nuts or seeds, dried fruit (raisins, cranberries), and cereal or pretzels, plus a few chocolate chips. Store it in a jar or baggies for easy snacks. (Skip nuts if anyone has an allergy.)"],
  ["how do i make ice pops | homemade popsicles | how to make popsicles", "Pour fruit juice, a smoothie or blended fruit with yogurt into popsicle molds or small paper cups. Add sticks (cover with foil to hold them up) and freeze for at least 4–6 hours."],
  ["how do i make iced tea | homemade iced tea | how to brew iced tea", "Steep 4–6 tea bags in 4 cups hot water for 4–5 minutes, then remove the bags. Add sugar or honey while it's warm if you like, then pour in 4 cups cold water and chill. Serve over ice with lemon."],
  ["how long do i cook spaghetti | spaghetti cooking time | how long does pasta take", "Most dried spaghetti takes about **8–10 minutes** in boiling salted water. Check the box for the exact time and taste a piece a minute before — it should be tender but still a little firm (al dente)."],
  ["how long do i bake cookies | cookie baking time | how do i know cookies are done", "Most drop cookies bake at 350°F (175°C) for 8–12 minutes. They're done when the edges are golden and the centers look just set — they firm up as they cool on the tray."],
  ["how long to cook a turkey | turkey cooking time | roasting a turkey", "A rough guide is about 13–15 minutes per pound at 325°F (165°C) for an unstuffed turkey. The turkey is safe when a thermometer in the thickest part of the thigh reads **165°F (74°C)**. Let it rest 20–30 minutes before carving."],
  ["how do i preheat the oven | what does preheat mean | preheat oven", "Preheating means turning the oven on to the recipe's temperature before you put the food in. Most ovens take 10–15 minutes and beep or turn off a light when ready. Baked goods rise and cook properly only in a fully heated oven."],
  ["how do i measure flour | measuring flour correctly | how to measure ingredients", "Spoon flour into a measuring cup until it's heaped, then level it off with the flat side of a knife — don't scoop or pack it down. For liquids, use a clear measuring jug and read it at eye level."],
  ["what does al dente mean | al dente meaning", "Al dente is Italian for to the tooth. It means pasta is cooked until tender but still has a slight firmness when you bite it — not mushy."],
  ["what does simmer mean | difference between boil and simmer | how to simmer", "A **boil** has big, rolling bubbles. A **simmer** is gentler — small bubbles rising slowly at the edges. To simmer, bring the liquid to a boil, then turn the heat down to low or medium-low."],
  ["what can i substitute for eggs in baking | egg substitute | no eggs for baking", "For one egg, try: 1/4 cup mashed banana, 1/4 cup applesauce, or 1 tbsp ground flaxseed mixed with 3 tbsp water (let it sit 5 minutes). These work best in muffins, pancakes and brownies."],
  ["what can i substitute for buttermilk | buttermilk substitute | no buttermilk", "Add 1 tablespoon lemon juice or white vinegar to a measuring cup, then fill with milk to 1 cup. Let it sit 5 minutes and it'll work just like buttermilk."],
  ["how do i chop an onion | cutting onions without crying | dice an onion", "Cut the onion in half through the root, peel it, and lay it flat. Make slices toward the root (not through it), then cut across them to dice. To cry less, use a sharp knife and chill the onion first. Always curl your fingertips away from the blade."],
  ["how do i use a knife safely | knife safety | cutting safely in the kitchen", "Knife safety tips:\n1. Use a sharp knife — dull ones slip more.\n2. Cut on a steady cutting board (put a damp towel underneath).\n3. Tuck your fingertips in like a claw.\n4. Cut away from your body and never try to catch a falling knife.\nKids should use knives only with an adult's help."],
  ["kitchen safety tips | how to be safe in the kitchen | cooking safety", "Kitchen safety basics:\n1. Wash hands before cooking and after touching raw meat or eggs.\n2. Turn pot handles inward so they can't be knocked.\n3. Use oven mitts for anything hot.\n4. Never leave the stove on unattended.\n5. Clean spills right away so no one slips.\nKids should cook with an adult nearby."],
  ["what do i do if there is a grease fire | grease fire | pan on fire | how to put out a kitchen fire", "Never pour water on a grease fire! Turn off the heat if you safely can, and cover the pan with a metal lid or baking sheet to smother it. If the fire spreads or you're unsure, get everyone out and call emergency services right away."],
  ["can i put metal in the microwave | microwave safety | what not to put in the microwave", "No — metal, foil and dishes with metallic trim can spark and cause a fire. Also avoid sealed containers and whole eggs (they can explode). Use microwave-safe glass or ceramic, and be careful of steam when uncovering food."],
  ["how do i prevent food poisoning | food safety tips | how to keep food safe", "Remember **clean, separate, cook, chill**: wash hands and surfaces, keep raw meat away from ready-to-eat food, cook foods to safe temperatures, and refrigerate leftovers within about 2 hours."],
  ["how long do leftovers last | how long can i keep leftovers | leftovers in the fridge", "Most cooked leftovers are good for about **3–4 days** in the fridge. Store them in sealed containers and reheat until steaming hot. When in doubt, throw it out."],
  ["how do i thaw frozen meat | defrosting chicken | how to defrost food safely", "The safest way is in the fridge overnight (larger items take longer). You can also seal it in a bag and put it in cold water, changing the water every 30 minutes, or use the microwave's defrost setting and cook right away. Don't thaw meat on the counter."],
  ["how do i wash fruits and vegetables | washing produce | should i wash fruit", "Rinse fruits and veggies under cool running water just before eating, rubbing them with your hands. Use a clean brush for firm items like potatoes and carrots. No soap needed!"],
  ["how do i tell if an egg is fresh | egg float test | is my egg bad", "Put the egg in a bowl of water. If it sinks and lies flat, it's fresh. If it stands up on end, it's older but still okay to use soon. If it floats, throw it out."],
  ["how do i know if an avocado is ripe | ripe avocado | how to ripen an avocado", "A ripe avocado gives slightly when you gently squeeze it. If it's rock hard, leave it on the counter for a few days — putting it in a paper bag with a banana speeds things up."],
  ["how do i cut a mango | cutting a mango | how to eat a mango", "Stand the mango up and slice down on each side of the flat pit to get two cheeks. Score each cheek in a grid without cutting through the skin, then push the skin up so the cubes pop out and slice them off."],
  ["how do i make cookies without an oven | no bake cookies | no-bake treats", "No-bake oat cookies: boil 1/2 cup butter, 2 cups sugar, 1/2 cup milk and 1/4 cup cocoa for 1 minute. Remove from heat and stir in 1/2 cup peanut butter, 1 tsp vanilla and 3 cups quick oats. Drop spoonfuls onto wax paper and let them set. Have an adult help with the hot mixture."],
  ["what should i cook for dinner | easy dinner ideas | quick dinner ideas", "Some easy dinner ideas: pasta with sauce and a side salad, tacos, stir-fry with rice, breakfast-for-dinner (eggs and toast), baked potatoes with toppings, homemade pizza, or soup with grilled cheese."],
  ["what should i eat for breakfast | healthy breakfast ideas | quick breakfast", "Good breakfast ideas: oatmeal with fruit, eggs and toast, yogurt with granola, a smoothie, whole-grain cereal with milk, or peanut butter on toast with a banana. Including some protein helps you stay full longer."],
  ["what is a healthy snack | healthy snack ideas | good snacks", "Healthy snacks: apple slices with peanut butter, carrots and hummus, yogurt, cheese and crackers, a handful of nuts, popcorn, a banana, or trail mix. Pairing fruit or veg with a little protein keeps you fuller."],
  ["what should i pack for lunch | school lunch ideas | lunchbox ideas", "A balanced lunch has a main (sandwich, wrap, pasta salad or leftovers), a fruit, a veggie, a protein or dairy (cheese, yogurt, hummus) and water. Pack an ice pack to keep things cold."],
  ["how do i make a stir fry | stir fry recipe | easy stir fry", "Stir-fry:\n1. Cut veggies and a protein (chicken, tofu or shrimp) into small pieces.\n2. Heat oil in a big pan on high and cook the protein until done, then set aside.\n3. Stir-fry the veggies 3–5 minutes so they stay crisp.\n4. Add the protein back with soy sauce, garlic and a little honey, and serve over rice or noodles."],
  ["how do i cook ramen better | upgrade instant noodles | instant ramen ideas", "To upgrade instant ramen: use half the seasoning packet, add a soft-boiled egg, some frozen veggies or spinach, sliced green onion and a splash of soy sauce or a little peanut butter. Leftover chicken works great too."],
  ["how do i make a fruit salad | fruit salad recipe", "Chop a mix of fruits — strawberries, grapes, melon, pineapple, apples, blueberries, kiwi — into bite-size pieces. Toss with a squeeze of lemon or orange juice so it stays fresh. Chill before serving."],
  ["how do i make garlic bread | garlic bread recipe", "Mix soft butter with minced garlic (or garlic powder), a pinch of salt and some parsley. Spread it on sliced bread or a split baguette and bake at 400°F (200°C) for 8–10 minutes until golden. Add cheese for extra yum."],
  ["how do i make whipped cream | homemade whipped cream", "Pour cold heavy cream into a cold bowl, add a little sugar and a drop of vanilla, and beat with a mixer until soft peaks form (2–4 minutes). Stop before it gets grainy or it'll turn into butter!"],
  ["how do i make cookies softer | chewy cookies tips | why are my cookies flat", "For softer, chewier cookies, use more brown sugar than white, chill the dough for 30 minutes, and take them out when the centers look a bit underdone. Flat cookies often mean the butter was too warm or melted."],
  ["how do i make a grilled cheese better | grilled cheese tips", "Use low heat so the cheese melts before the bread burns, mix two cheeses (like cheddar and mozzarella), and try spreading mayo on the outside instead of butter for an extra-golden crust. Adding tomato or ham is a nice twist."],
  ["how do i hard boil eggs so they peel easily | eggs hard to peel | easy peel eggs", "Use eggs that are a week or more old, start them in already-boiling water, and plunge them into ice water right after cooking. Crack the shell all over and peel under running water starting at the wide end."],
  ["how do i make pasta sauce | easy tomato sauce | homemade spaghetti sauce", "Cook a little chopped onion and garlic in olive oil until soft, add a can of crushed tomatoes, a pinch of salt and some dried basil or oregano, and simmer 15–20 minutes. Toss with pasta and top with Parmesan."],
  ["how do i cook frozen vegetables | frozen veggies | best way to cook frozen vegetables", "Frozen veggies are quick! Microwave them in a covered bowl with a splash of water for 3–5 minutes, or sauté them in a hot pan with a little oil until heated through. Season with salt, pepper, butter or garlic."],
  ["how do i make a bowl of cereal | how to make cereal", "Pour cereal into a bowl first, then add milk until it comes up just below the top of the cereal. Eat it quickly before it gets soggy! (Cereal first is the usual way.)"],
  ["how do i make a hot dog | how to cook hot dogs", "Hot dogs are already cooked, so you just need to heat them: simmer in water for about 5 minutes, grill or pan-fry until browned, or microwave 30–60 seconds. Serve in a bun with your favorite toppings. For little kids, cut them lengthwise to prevent choking."],
  ["how do i make cupcakes | cupcake recipe", "Use a cake recipe (like vanilla cake) and fill paper-lined muffin cups about 2/3 full. Bake at 350°F (175°C) for 18–22 minutes until a toothpick comes out clean. Cool completely before frosting."],
  ["how do i make frosting | buttercream recipe | easy icing", "Beat 1/2 cup soft butter until creamy, then gradually add 2 cups powdered sugar, 1–2 tbsp milk and 1 tsp vanilla. Beat until fluffy. Add a few drops of food coloring for fun colors."],
  ["how do i cook an egg in the microwave | microwave scrambled eggs | microwave egg", "Whisk 2 eggs with a splash of milk in a microwave-safe mug, then microwave 30 seconds, stir, and microwave 20–30 seconds more until just set. Don't microwave eggs in their shell — they can burst."],
  ["how do i make a milkshake | milkshake recipe", "Blend 2–3 scoops of ice cream with about 1/2 cup milk until smooth. Add chocolate syrup, strawberries or a banana for flavor. Less milk makes it thicker."],
  ["how much water should i drink | how much water a day | daily water intake", "A common guide is around 6–8 cups a day for kids and about 8 or more for adults, plus more when it's hot or you're active. A simple check: if your pee is pale yellow, you're probably drinking enough."],
  ["how can i drink more water | tips to drink more water | i forget to drink water", "Keep a refillable water bottle with you, drink a glass with every meal, add lemon, cucumber or berries for flavor, and set reminders. Eating watery foods like fruit and soup helps too."],
  ["what are signs of dehydration | am i dehydrated | dehydration symptoms", "Common signs are thirst, dark yellow pee, a dry mouth, headache, tiredness and feeling dizzy. Drink some water and rest somewhere cool. If someone is confused, very dizzy or faints, get help from an adult or call emergency services."],
  ["how do i eat healthy | healthy eating tips | how to eat better", "Healthy eating basics:\n1. Fill half your plate with fruits and veggies.\n2. Choose whole grains like oats and brown rice.\n3. Include protein — beans, eggs, fish, chicken, nuts.\n4. Drink water instead of sugary drinks most of the time.\n5. Treats are fine sometimes — balance is the goal."],
  ["what is a balanced meal | balanced plate | what goes on a healthy plate", "A balanced meal has about half veggies and fruit, a quarter protein (like chicken, fish, beans or eggs) and a quarter grains or starch (like rice, pasta or bread), plus a drink of water or milk."],
  ["how do i get my kid to eat vegetables | picky eater tips | i don't like vegetables", "Try veggies a different way — roasted, raw with dip, blended into sauces or smoothies. Offer small tastes again and again (it can take many tries), let kids help cook, and pair new foods with favorites."],
  ["why is breakfast important | should i eat breakfast | is breakfast the most important meal", "Breakfast gives your body and brain fuel after a long night, which can help with energy and focus in the morning. It doesn't have to be big — even yogurt and fruit or toast with peanut butter helps."],
  ["is sugar bad for you | how much sugar is too much | too much sugar", "A little sugar is fine, but lots of it — especially from soda, candy and sweet drinks — isn't great for your teeth and energy levels. Try saving sweets for treats and drinking water most of the time."],
  ["what are good sources of protein | protein foods | foods with protein", "Great protein sources include eggs, chicken, fish, beans, lentils, tofu, Greek yogurt, milk, cheese, nuts, seeds and peanut butter. Spreading protein through the day keeps you fuller."],
  ["how do i sleep better | tips for better sleep | how to get good sleep", "Sleep tips:\n1. Go to bed and wake up at about the same time every day.\n2. Put screens away 30–60 minutes before bed.\n3. Keep your room cool, dark and quiet.\n4. Avoid caffeine in the afternoon and evening.\n5. Try a calming routine: shower, read, stretch."],
  ["i can't fall asleep | i can't sleep | how to fall asleep fast | trouble falling asleep", "Try slow breathing (in for 4, hold for 4, out for 6), relaxing each muscle from your toes upward, or imagining a calm place in detail. If you're still awake after about 20 minutes, get up and do something quiet in dim light, then try again. If sleep troubles keep happening, talk to a parent or doctor."],
  ["how much sleep do i need | how many hours of sleep | how much sleep do kids need", "General guides: kids 6–12 need about 9–12 hours, teens about 8–10 hours, and adults about 7–9 hours a night. Feeling sleepy during the day is a sign you might need more."],
  ["how do i wake up early | how to become a morning person | waking up early tips", "Move your bedtime earlier by 15 minutes every few days, put your alarm across the room so you have to get up, open the curtains for sunlight right away and have something to look forward to in the morning."],
  ["should i take a nap | are naps good | how long should a nap be", "A short nap of 10–20 minutes can boost energy without making you groggy. Try to nap earlier in the afternoon so it doesn't mess up your nighttime sleep."],
  ["what should i do if i have a nightmare | bad dreams | scared after a nightmare", "Remind yourself it was just a dream and you're safe. Take slow breaths, turn on a soft light, get a sip of water, or think about something happy. If nightmares happen a lot, it helps to tell a parent or trusted adult."],
  ["exercise ideas | how do i get more exercise | ways to stay active", "Fun ways to move: walking or biking, dancing to music, jumping rope, swimming, playing tag or a sport, hiking, yoga, or a quick at-home workout. Kids should aim for about an hour of activity a day; adults about 150 minutes a week."],
  ["exercises i can do at home | home workout | workout without equipment", "Try this no-equipment circuit, 30 seconds each, 2–3 rounds:\n1. Jumping jacks\n2. Squats\n3. Push-ups (on knees is fine)\n4. Lunges\n5. Plank\n6. High knees\nWarm up first and drink water!"],
  ["why is exercise good for you | benefits of exercise | is exercise important", "Exercise strengthens your heart, muscles and bones, boosts your mood, helps you sleep better, and can make it easier to focus. Even short bursts of movement add up."],
  ["how do i stretch | stretching routine | how to warm up before exercise", "Warm up with 5 minutes of easy movement like marching or arm circles. After exercise, hold gentle stretches for 15–30 seconds each — hamstrings, quads, calves, shoulders. Stretch until you feel a pull, never pain."],
  ["how do i start running | running for beginners | how to get better at running", "Start with walk–run intervals: run 1 minute, walk 2 minutes, for about 20 minutes. Slowly increase the running time each week. Go at a pace where you can still talk, wear comfy shoes and drink water."],
  ["how do i do a push up | push up form | proper push up", "Place hands a bit wider than your shoulders, keep your body in a straight line from head to heels, lower your chest toward the floor, then push back up. Start on your knees if full push-ups are too hard."],
  ["how do i stay focused | how to concentrate | i can't focus | trouble concentrating", "Focus tips:\n1. Put your phone in another room or turn on Do Not Disturb.\n2. Work in short bursts — 25 minutes, then a 5-minute break.\n3. Write down one clear goal before you start.\n4. Keep your desk tidy and have water nearby.\n5. Move or stretch on breaks to reset your brain."],
  ["what is the pomodoro technique | pomodoro method | 25 minute study method", "The Pomodoro Technique: set a timer for 25 minutes and work on one task with no distractions. Then take a 5-minute break. After 4 rounds, take a longer 15–30 minute break. It makes big tasks feel more manageable."],
  ["how do i stop procrastinating | i keep putting things off | procrastination tips", "Start with just 5 minutes — getting started is the hardest part. Break the task into tiny steps, do the easiest one first, remove distractions and reward yourself when you finish. Being kind to yourself works better than guilt!"],
  ["how do i stop getting distracted by my phone | phone distraction | too much screen time", "Put your phone out of sight while working, turn off non-urgent notifications, use focus or screen-time settings, and set specific times to check messages. Swapping some screen time for a hobby or going outside helps too."],
  ["study tips | how do i study better | best way to study | how to study effectively", "Study tips that really work:\n1. **Test yourself** instead of just rereading — flashcards and practice questions.\n2. Space it out: short sessions over several days beat one long cram.\n3. Explain the topic out loud as if teaching someone.\n4. Mix up different types of problems.\n5. Sleep well — your brain stores memories while you sleep."],
  ["how do i prepare for a test | how to study for a test | test prep tips | studying for an exam", "To prepare for a test:\n1. Find out what topics it covers.\n2. Start reviewing a few days early, a little each day.\n3. Make flashcards or a one-page summary.\n4. Do practice problems and quiz yourself.\n5. Get a good night's sleep and eat breakfast on test day."],
  ["how do i not get nervous before a test | test anxiety | i'm nervous about my exam", "A bit of nerves is normal and can even help you focus! Prepare early so you feel ready, take slow deep breaths before you start, read each question carefully, and skip hard ones to come back later. Remind yourself one test doesn't define you."],
  ["test taking tips | how to do well on a test | tips during a test", "During the test: read the directions carefully, answer the easy questions first, keep an eye on the time, underline key words, eliminate wrong choices on multiple choice, and save a few minutes to check your work."],
  ["how do i memorize things | memory tricks | how to remember things for school", "Memory tricks: make up an acronym or silly sentence (mnemonic), chunk info into small groups, draw pictures or diagrams, connect new facts to things you know, and review them a few times spread out over days."],
  ["what are flashcards | how to use flashcards | making flashcards", "Write a question or term on one side of a card and the answer on the other. Quiz yourself, putting the cards you get right in one pile and the tricky ones in another to review more often. Say answers out loud before flipping!"],
  ["how do i take notes | note taking tips | how to take good notes", "Good notes tips:\n1. Don't write everything — capture key ideas and examples.\n2. Use your own words, abbreviations and bullet points.\n3. Leave space to add things later.\n4. Highlight or star what the teacher repeats.\n5. Review and tidy your notes the same day."],
  ["what is the cornell note taking method | cornell notes | cornell method", "Divide your page into three parts: a wide right column for notes during class, a narrow left column for key questions or keywords, and a bottom section for a short summary. Later, cover the right side and quiz yourself using the left-side cues."],
  ["how do i do my homework faster | homework tips | how to get homework done", "Homework tips:\n1. Pick a regular time and quiet spot.\n2. Make a quick list and start with the hardest or most urgent task.\n3. Put your phone away.\n4. Take short breaks between subjects.\n5. Pack everything in your bag as soon as you finish."],
  ["i don't understand my homework | i'm stuck on homework | help with homework i don't get", "Reread the question slowly and underline what it's asking. Look back at your notes or textbook examples, try a similar easier problem, and write down exactly where you get stuck. Then ask a teacher, parent, classmate or tutor — asking for help is smart, not weak."],
  ["i have too much homework | overwhelmed with homework | so much schoolwork", "Write down everything you need to do with due dates, then sort by what's due first. Break big assignments into small steps and do a little each day. If it's truly too much, talk to your teacher — they often can help."],
  ["how do i write an essay | essay tips | how to start an essay", "Essay steps:\n1. Understand the prompt and pick your main point (thesis).\n2. Brainstorm and make a simple outline.\n3. Write an intro, body paragraphs (one idea each with examples) and a conclusion.\n4. Revise for clarity, then check spelling and grammar."],
  ["how do i write a paragraph | paragraph structure | parts of a paragraph", "A strong paragraph has a **topic sentence** that states the main idea, 2–4 supporting sentences with details or examples, and a closing sentence that wraps it up or leads to the next idea."],
  ["how do i get better at reading | reading tips | how to read faster", "Read a little every day on topics you enjoy — comics, magazines and audiobooks count too. Look up unfamiliar words, summarize each chapter in a sentence, and try reading aloud. Speed comes naturally with practice."],
  ["how do i get better at math | math tips | i'm bad at math", "Nobody is just bad at math — it's a skill that grows with practice! Do a few problems every day, show all your steps, check mistakes to learn why they happened, and ask questions as soon as something confuses you. Real-life math like cooking and money helps too."],
  ["how do i memorize multiplication tables | times tables tips | learn times tables", "Learn the easy ones first (1s, 2s, 5s, 10s), then use tricks: 9s fingers trick, doubles for 4s (double, then double again), and the fact that 3×7 is the same as 7×3. Practice a few minutes daily with flashcards or games."],
  ["how do i give a presentation | presentation tips | public speaking tips", "Presentation tips:\n1. Know your topic and practice out loud several times.\n2. Use note cards with keywords, not full sentences.\n3. Look at your audience and speak slowly.\n4. Stand tall and take a deep breath before starting.\n5. If you make a mistake, just keep going — no one minds!"],
  ["how do i do a group project | working in a group | group project tips", "Agree on the goal early, split up tasks fairly based on strengths, set mini deadlines, and check in regularly. Listen to everyone's ideas and if someone isn't helping, talk to them kindly first, then your teacher if needed."],
  ["how do i organize my backpack | messy backpack | school organization tips", "Empty your backpack once a week, use a folder for each subject, keep a pencil pouch for supplies, and have one folder for papers going home. Pack it the night before so mornings are calm."],
  ["how do i manage my time | time management tips | how to manage time better", "Time management tips:\n1. Write your tasks down — a list or planner.\n2. Do the most important thing first.\n3. Break big tasks into small steps with deadlines.\n4. Estimate how long things take, then add a little extra.\n5. Schedule breaks and fun too!"],
  ["how do i make a to do list | to-do list tips | how to make a checklist", "Write down everything you need to do, then pick your top 3 most important tasks for today. Keep tasks small and specific (Read chapter 4 instead of Study). Crossing things off feels great and keeps you going."],
  ["how do i use a planner | planner tips | how to make a schedule", "Write down due dates and events as soon as you hear about them. Each evening, look at tomorrow's plan; each weekend, look at the week ahead. Color-code by subject or activity so it's easy to scan."],
  ["how do i set goals | goal setting | how to achieve my goals", "Make goals **SMART**: Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant and Time-bound. For example, Read 20 minutes every night this month. Break it into small steps, track your progress and celebrate milestones."],
  ["how do i build a good habit | how to make a habit stick | new habits", "Start tiny (2 minutes a day), attach it to something you already do (after I brush my teeth, I'll read one page), track it on a calendar and don't break the chain. Skipping once is fine — just get back on track."],
  ["how do i break a bad habit | stop a bad habit | quitting a habit", "Notice what triggers the habit, then replace it with something better instead of just stopping. Make the bad habit harder to do (like moving your phone away) and be patient — habits take a few weeks to change."],
  ["how do i get motivated | i have no motivation | how to motivate myself", "Motivation often comes *after* you start, so begin with a tiny step. Remind yourself why it matters, picture how good you'll feel when done, play some music, and reward yourself afterward. Rest if you're truly exhausted."],
  ["how do i get ready for school faster | morning routine | how to have a good morning", "Prepare the night before: lay out clothes, pack your bag and lunch. In the morning, follow the same order each day — wake up, wash up, dress, eat, brush teeth, go. A checklist on the door helps a lot."],
  ["what is a good bedtime routine | bedtime routine ideas | night routine", "A calming bedtime routine might be: tidy up for 5 minutes, take a shower, brush your teeth, put on pajamas, read a book or listen to calm music, then lights out at the same time each night. No screens in the last half hour helps."],
  ["how do i brush my teeth properly | brushing teeth | how long to brush teeth", "Brush twice a day for **2 minutes** with a pea-sized amount of fluoride toothpaste. Use small circles, cover the outside, inside and chewing surfaces, and brush your tongue gently. Floss once a day too."],
  ["how often should i wash my hands | how to wash hands properly | hand washing", "Wash with soap and water for at least **20 seconds** (about two rounds of Happy Birthday) — palms, backs, between fingers and under nails. Do it before eating, after the bathroom, after blowing your nose and after touching pets or raw food."],
  ["how do i stop biting my nails | nail biting | i bite my nails", "Keep nails short and filed, keep your hands busy with a stress ball or fidget, notice when you do it (often when bored or nervous) and reward yourself for progress. Some people use a bitter-tasting nail polish as a reminder."],
  ["how do i stop hiccups | hiccups cure | get rid of hiccups", "Try sipping cold water slowly, holding your breath for about 10 seconds, swallowing a spoonful of sugar, or breathing slowly into cupped hands. Most hiccups go away on their own within a few minutes."],
  ["what do i do when i'm bored | i'm bored | things to do when bored", "Ideas: read a book, draw or doodle, build something with blocks or cardboard, learn a magic trick, go for a walk or bike ride, bake something, write a story, do a puzzle, organize your room or call a friend."],
  ["how do i make friends | how to make new friends | making friends tips", "Tips for making friends:\n1. Smile and say hi — small hellos add up.\n2. Ask questions and really listen to the answers.\n3. Join a club, team or activity you enjoy.\n4. Look for someone sitting alone and invite them in.\n5. Be yourself — the right friends will like the real you."],
  ["how do i make friends at a new school | i'm new at school | first day at a new school", "Being new is hard, but it gets easier! Introduce yourself to the people near you, ask someone to show you around, sit with a group at lunch, and join a club or sport. Teachers and counselors are also happy to help you meet people."],
  ["how do i start a conversation | what do i say to someone new | conversation starters", "Try a friendly question about something you share: What did you think of that game?, What are you reading?, or Have you seen any good movies lately? Then listen and ask follow-up questions. Most people love talking about their interests."],
  ["how do i be a good friend | what makes a good friend | being a good friend", "Good friends listen, keep promises, are honest but kind, celebrate each other's wins, say sorry when they mess up, stick up for each other, and give each other space when needed."],
  ["i had a fight with my friend | how do i fix a friendship | my friend is mad at me", "Give it a little time to cool down, then talk calmly in private. Listen to their side, explain how you felt using I statements, and apologize for your part. Most friendships can be fixed with honesty and kindness."],
  ["how do i say sorry | how to apologize | how to give a good apology", "A good apology:\n1. Say what you did wrong: I'm sorry I took your pencil without asking.\n2. Show you understand how it made them feel.\n3. Say how you'll do better next time.\n4. Don't add but — just own it."],
  ["what do i do if i'm being bullied | i'm being bullied | someone is bullying me", "Being bullied is never your fault. Try to stay calm, tell the bully firmly to stop, and walk away to where adults are. Most importantly, **tell a trusted adult** — a parent, teacher or school counselor — and keep telling until someone helps. Stay close to friends too."],
  ["what do i do if i see someone being bullied | how to help someone who is bullied | bystander", "You can help by not joining in or laughing, being kind to the person afterward, inviting them to sit with you, and telling a teacher or trusted adult. Only step in directly if it's safe."],
  ["how do i deal with stress | stress relief tips | i'm stressed out | how to relax", "Ways to handle stress:\n1. Take slow, deep breaths.\n2. Move your body — a walk, a dance, a stretch.\n3. Break big worries into small steps.\n4. Talk to someone you trust.\n5. Do something calming like reading, music or drawing.\nIf stress feels overwhelming or won't go away, talk to a trusted adult or a professional."],
  ["how do i calm down when i'm angry | i'm so angry | anger management tips", "When anger hits: stop, step away and take 5 slow breaths. Count to 10, squeeze a pillow, or go for a quick walk. Once you're calmer, put the feeling into words: I feel angry because... Talking it out with a trusted adult helps, especially if anger happens a lot."],
  ["how do i deal with being nervous | i feel nervous | how to stop being nervous", "Nerves are your body getting ready for something important. Try box breathing (in 4, hold 4, out 4, hold 4), picture things going well, and remind yourself you've handled hard things before. Practicing ahead of time helps a lot."],
  ["what is box breathing | breathing exercise | calming breathing technique", "Box breathing: breathe in slowly for 4 counts, hold for 4, breathe out for 4, and hold for 4. Repeat 4–5 times. It helps calm your body when you feel stressed, angry or nervous."],
  ["i feel sad | i'm feeling down | what to do when you're sad", "I'm sorry you're feeling down. It can help to talk to someone you trust, get some fresh air, listen to music you like, or do something kind for yourself. Feelings do pass. If you've felt sad for a long time, please talk to a parent, school counselor or doctor — you don't have to handle it alone."],
  ["i feel lonely | i have no friends | nobody likes me", "Feeling lonely is really hard, and lots of people feel it sometimes. Try reaching out to one person — a classmate, cousin or neighbor — or join a club around something you love. Talking to a trusted adult can help too. You matter, and there are people who'd be glad to know you."],
  ["how do i deal with worry | i worry too much | anxious thoughts", "Try writing your worries down, then ask: Is this likely? What can I actually do about it? Focus on one small step you can control. Breathing exercises and exercise help too. If worry is getting in the way of daily life, talk to a trusted adult or a professional."],
  ["how do i handle making a mistake | i messed up | i made a mistake", "Everyone makes mistakes — that's how we learn! Own it, fix what you can, apologize if someone was affected, and think about what you'd do differently next time. Then let it go; be as kind to yourself as you would be to a friend."],
  ["how do i deal with losing a game | i hate losing | being a good sport", "It's okay to feel disappointed! Take a breath, shake hands or say good game, and think about one thing you can practice for next time. Being a good sport makes people want to play with you again."],
  ["how do i build confidence | how to be more confident | low self esteem", "Build confidence by trying small new things, practicing skills you care about, standing tall, and noticing what you do well (write it down!). Try talking to yourself the way you'd talk to a good friend. Confidence grows with practice."],
  ["how do i say no to someone | how to say no politely | peer pressure", "You can say no kindly but clearly: No thanks, I'm not into that. You don't owe a long explanation. If friends keep pushing you to do something unsafe or wrong, walk away and talk to a trusted adult. Real friends respect your no."],
  ["good manners | what are good manners | basic manners | etiquette tips", "Everyday good manners: say please and thank you, greet people and look them in the eye, don't interrupt, hold the door, clean up after yourself, wait your turn, and apologize when you bump into someone."],
  ["table manners | how to have good table manners | dinner etiquette", "Table manners: wash your hands first, put your napkin on your lap, wait until everyone is served, chew with your mouth closed, ask for things to be passed instead of reaching, don't talk with food in your mouth, and say thank you for the meal."],
  ["how do i introduce myself | how to introduce yourself | meeting someone new", "Smile, make eye contact and say: Hi, I'm [your name]. Nice to meet you! If it fits, offer a handshake and add something about yourself, like I'm in Ms. Lee's class. Then ask their name and try to use it."],
  ["how do i shake hands | proper handshake | handshake tips", "Use your right hand, grip firmly but not too hard, shake 2–3 times, look the person in the eye and smile. Say something like Nice to meet you."],
  ["phone manners | how to answer the phone politely | phone etiquette", "Answer with a friendly Hello! If it's for someone else, say Just a moment, please and get them. If you take a message, write down the name, number and reason. Keep your voice low in public places."],
  ["how do i be polite online | internet etiquette | netiquette", "Be kind online like you would face to face: think before you post, don't share other people's photos or info without asking, avoid ALL CAPS (it reads as yelling) and never share passwords or personal details with strangers."],
  ["how do i be a good guest | manners at a friend's house | sleepover manners", "Say hello to your friend's family, follow their house rules, offer to help clean up, try the food that's served, and say thank you before you leave. A thank-you note afterward is a lovely touch."],
  ["how do i write a thank you note | thank you card | what to write in a thank you note", "A simple thank-you note:\n1. Greeting: Dear Aunt Maya,\n2. Thank them for the specific gift or kindness.\n3. Say how you'll use it or why it meant a lot.\n4. Add a warm closing: Love, or Thanks again,\n5. Sign your name."],
  ["thank you note example | sample thank you letter | thank you card example", "Example: Dear Grandpa, Thank you so much for the puzzle book! I've already finished three puzzles and I'm saving the hardest for our next visit. Thanks for always thinking of me. Love, Sam"],
  ["how do i write a letter | how to write a friendly letter | letter format", "A friendly letter has five parts: the date, a greeting (Dear Jamie,), the body with your news and questions, a closing (Your friend,) and your signature. Address the envelope with the receiver's address in the middle and yours in the top-left corner."],
  ["how do i write an email | email etiquette | how to write a polite email", "Write a clear subject line, start with a greeting (Hi Mr. Patel,), keep it short and to the point, say please and thank you, end with a closing (Thanks, Ava) and reread it before you press send."],
  ["how do i save money | money saving tips | tips for saving money", "Saving tips:\n1. Save first — put some money away as soon as you get it.\n2. Set a goal so you know what you're saving for.\n3. Use a jar, piggy bank or savings account.\n4. Wait a day before buying things you want.\n5. Compare prices and avoid buying on impulse."],
  ["what is a budget | how do i make a budget | budgeting basics", "A budget is a plan for your money. List what money comes in (allowance, pay), then plan what goes out: things you need, things you want, and savings. A simple rule some people use is to split money into **save, spend and share** jars."],
  ["what is interest | how does interest work | what is compound interest", "Interest is money paid for using money. If you save in a bank, the bank pays *you* interest. If you borrow, *you* pay interest. Compound interest means you earn interest on your interest too, so savings grow faster over time."],
  ["what is a bank account | how does a bank account work | why have a bank account", "A bank account is a safe place to keep money at a bank. You can put money in (deposit), take it out (withdraw), and keep track of it. A **savings account** is for saving and often earns interest; a **checking account** is for everyday spending."],
  ["what is the difference between a need and a want | needs vs wants", "A **need** is something you must have to live and be safe — food, water, shelter, basic clothing. A **want** is something nice to have but not necessary, like toys, games or fancy shoes. Taking care of needs first is a key money skill."],
  ["how do i earn money as a kid | ways for kids to make money | how can a teenager make money", "Kid-friendly ways to earn money (with a parent's okay): extra chores, a lemonade stand, pet sitting, walking dogs, raking leaves, washing cars, selling crafts or old toys, or helping neighbors with yard work."],
  ["what is a debit card vs credit card | difference between debit and credit | credit card meaning", "A **debit card** spends money already in your bank account. A **credit card** borrows money you must pay back later — and if you don't pay it in full, you're charged interest. Treat credit like a loan, not free money."],
  ["what is a loan | how do loans work", "A loan is money you borrow and agree to pay back over time, usually with interest added. That means you pay back more than you borrowed, so it's wise to borrow only what you really need."],
  ["how do i save up for something | saving for a goal | how to save for something i want", "Pick your goal and find out how much it costs. Decide how much you can save each week, then divide to see how many weeks it'll take. Track your progress on a chart and resist small impulse buys along the way!"],
  ["what is an allowance | should kids get an allowance | how to spend allowance", "An allowance is money given regularly, often for helping out at home. It's a great way to practice budgeting: try saving part, spending part and sharing part with a cause you care about."],
  ["how do i count change | counting money | how to make change", "Start from the price and count up to the amount paid. For example, if something costs $3.65 and you pay $5: add 10¢ to get $3.75, a quarter to $4.00, and $1 to $5.00 — that's $1.35 change."],
  ["how do i clean my room | cleaning my room fast | how to tidy my room", "Speed-clean your room:\n1. Put on music and set a timer for 15 minutes.\n2. Throw away trash first.\n3. Put dirty clothes in the hamper.\n4. Put things back where they belong.\n5. Make your bed and clear surfaces.\n6. Vacuum or sweep if there's time."],
  ["how do i organize my room | room organization ideas | messy room help", "Sort things into keep, donate and trash piles. Give everything a home — bins, shelves, hooks — and label them. Keep things you use most within easy reach. Do a 5-minute tidy each night so it never gets out of hand."],
  ["how do i make my bed | making the bed | how to make a bed neatly", "Pull the fitted sheet tight, straighten the top sheet and blanket so they hang evenly, fold the top edge back a little, fluff your pillows and place them at the head. It takes just 2 minutes and makes the whole room look tidier."],
  ["how do i do laundry | how to wash clothes | laundry for beginners", "Laundry basics:\n1. Sort clothes: lights, darks and delicates.\n2. Check pockets and read care labels.\n3. Add detergent (follow the bottle) and pick the right cycle — cold water works for most clothes.\n4. Move clothes to the dryer quickly or hang them up.\n5. Fold or hang them right away to avoid wrinkles."],
  ["how do i fold a shirt | folding clothes | how to fold a t-shirt", "Lay the shirt face down, fold each side (with the sleeve) toward the middle, then fold the bottom up to the collar — or in thirds for a smaller fold. Flip it over and it's neat and ready to stack."],
  ["how do i get a stain out | stain removal tips | how to remove stains from clothes", "Act fast! Blot (don't rub) the stain, rinse it from the back with cold water, rub in a little dish soap or laundry detergent, let it sit 10–15 minutes, then wash. Check that it's gone before drying — heat can set stains."],
  ["how do i get grass stains out | grass stain removal", "Pretreat with laundry detergent or a stain remover and gently scrub with an old toothbrush. Let it sit 15 minutes, then wash in the warmest water the label allows. Repeat before drying if it's still there."],
  ["how do i get ketchup out of clothes | tomato sauce stain", "Scrape off extra ketchup, rinse from the back with cold water, then rub in dish soap and let it sit a few minutes. Rinse and wash as usual. Don't use hot water first — it can set the stain."],
  ["how do i get gum out of clothes | gum stuck on clothes | gum in hair", "For clothes, put the item in the freezer (or rub with ice) until the gum hardens, then scrape it off with a dull knife. For hair, work a little peanut butter or cooking oil into the gum to loosen it, then comb it out and shampoo."],
  ["how do i iron a shirt | how to iron clothes | ironing tips", "Check the label for the right heat setting. Iron the collar first, then the cuffs and sleeves, then the body. Keep the iron moving so you don't scorch the fabric. Irons get very hot — kids should ask an adult for help."],
  ["how do i wash dishes by hand | washing dishes | dish washing tips", "Scrape food into the trash, fill the sink with hot soapy water, and wash the least greasy items first (glasses, then plates, then pots). Rinse in clean water and let them air dry in a rack. Be careful with sharp knives."],
  ["how do i load a dishwasher | dishwasher tips", "Put plates and big items on the bottom facing the center, cups and bowls upside down on the top rack, and silverware handles-down (knives handles-up). Scrape food off first and don't overcrowd so water can reach everything."],
  ["how do i clean a bathroom | bathroom cleaning tips", "Spray the sink, tub and toilet with cleaner and let it sit a few minutes. Wipe the mirror and counters, scrub the sink and tub, clean the toilet with a brush (and wipe the outside), then sweep and mop the floor. Never mix bleach with other cleaners."],
  ["what chores can kids do | chores for kids | age appropriate chores", "Kids can help a lot! Little kids can put toys away and feed pets. School-age kids can make their bed, set the table, sort laundry, water plants and take out trash. Older kids can vacuum, wash dishes, cook simple meals and do laundry."],
  ["how do i make chores fun | chores are boring", "Put on a playlist and race the clock, turn it into a game or contest, pair up with a family member, or reward yourself when you're done. Doing chores at the same time each day makes them feel automatic."],
  ["how do i declutter | decluttering tips | too much stuff", "Go one area at a time. For each item ask: Do I use it? Do I love it? If not, donate, sell or recycle it. Keep a donation box handy so getting rid of things is easy."],
  ["how do i sew a button | sewing a button back on | how to sew", "Thread a needle and knot the end. Push it up through the fabric and one hole of the button, then down through the opposite hole. Repeat 5–6 times, wrap the thread around the stitches under the button a few times, then knot it on the back."],
  ["how do i treat a small cut | first aid for a cut | what to do if i cut myself | scraped knee", "For a small cut or scrape:\n1. Wash your hands, then rinse the cut with clean water.\n2. Press gently with a clean cloth to stop bleeding.\n3. Put on a thin layer of antibiotic ointment if you have it.\n4. Cover with a bandage and change it daily.\nIf it's deep, won't stop bleeding or shows signs of infection, get medical help. **Call emergency services for serious injuries.**"],
  ["what do i do for a nosebleed | how to stop a nosebleed | my nose is bleeding", "Sit up and lean slightly **forward** (not back), then pinch the soft part of your nose for 10–15 minutes without letting go. Breathe through your mouth. If it won't stop after 20 minutes or came from a hard hit, get medical help."],
  ["how do i treat a burn | first aid for burns | i burned my hand", "For a small burn: run cool (not ice-cold) water over it for about **10–20 minutes**, take off rings or tight items nearby, and cover loosely with a clean non-stick bandage. Don't use ice, butter or pop blisters. For large, deep or face burns, call emergency services."],
  ["what do i do for a bee sting | bee sting first aid | i got stung by a bee", "Scrape the stinger out sideways with a fingernail or card, wash the area with soap and water, and use a cold pack to reduce swelling. If the person has trouble breathing, swelling of the face or throat, or feels faint, **call emergency services immediately**."],
  ["what do i do for a bruise | how to treat a bruise | bump on the head", "Put a cold pack wrapped in a cloth on the bruise for 10–15 minutes at a time and rest it. For a bump on the head, watch for vomiting, confusion, bad headache or sleepiness — if any happen, get medical help right away."],
  ["what do i do for a sprained ankle | twisted ankle | how to treat a sprain", "Remember **RICE**: Rest it, Ice it (wrapped, 15–20 minutes at a time), Compress with a bandage, and Elevate it above your heart. If you can't put weight on it or it looks deformed, see a doctor."],
  ["what do i do if someone is choking | choking first aid | someone can't breathe", "If someone can cough forcefully, encourage them to keep coughing. If they can't breathe, talk or cough, **call emergency services** right away and get an adult. Trained people can give back blows and abdominal thrusts — taking a first aid class is a great idea."],
  ["when should i call emergency services | when to call 911 | emergency number", "Call emergency services (like 911, 112 or 999 depending on your country) when someone is badly hurt, not breathing, unconscious, having a seizure, there's a fire, or you or someone is in danger. Stay calm, say where you are and what happened, and don't hang up until they tell you to."],
  ["what should be in a first aid kit | first aid kit list | build a first aid kit", "A basic first aid kit: adhesive bandages in several sizes, gauze pads, medical tape, antiseptic wipes, antibiotic ointment, tweezers, small scissors, a cold pack, disposable gloves, and a list of emergency numbers. Keep it where everyone knows."],
  ["how do i get a splinter out | splinter removal", "Wash your hands and the area. If the end sticks out, gently pull it out with clean tweezers in the same direction it went in. Wash again and cover with a bandage. If it's deep or under a nail, ask an adult or doctor for help."],
  ["what do i do for a sunburn | sunburn relief | how to prevent sunburn", "Cool the skin with a cool shower or damp cloth, apply aloe vera or a gentle moisturizer, drink extra water and stay out of the sun. Prevent it next time with sunscreen (SPF 30+), reapplied every 2 hours, plus a hat and shade."],
  ["what do i do for a bug bite | mosquito bite itch | stop itching bug bites", "Wash the bite with soap and water, put on a cold pack, and try not to scratch. Anti-itch cream or a paste of baking soda and water can help. If someone has trouble breathing or a big reaction, get medical help."],
  ["how do i take care of a dog | dog care basics | new puppy tips", "Dog care basics:\n1. Fresh water and healthy dog food every day.\n2. Daily walks and play.\n3. Regular brushing, nail trims and baths when needed.\n4. Vet checkups and vaccines.\n5. Lots of love and training with treats and praise.\nNever feed dogs chocolate, grapes, onions or xylitol."],
  ["how do i train my dog | dog training tips | teach my dog to sit", "Use short sessions (5–10 minutes) and lots of rewards. To teach sit: hold a treat near the dog's nose, move it up and back so their bottom lowers, say sit and give the treat right away. Be patient and consistent — never yell or hit."],
  ["how do i take care of a cat | cat care basics | new kitten tips", "Cat care basics: fresh water and cat food daily, a clean litter box (scoop every day), a scratching post, toys for play, a cozy place to sleep and regular vet visits. Brush long-haired cats often. Keep toxic plants like lilies away."],
  ["how do i take care of a fish | goldfish care | fish tank tips", "Fish care: use a properly sized tank with a filter, treat tap water with water conditioner, feed a small pinch once or twice a day (only what they eat in a couple of minutes), and change about 10–25% of the water each week."],
  ["how do i take care of a hamster | hamster care basics | pet hamster tips", "Hamsters need a roomy cage with deep bedding, a solid exercise wheel, a hideout, chew toys, fresh water and hamster food plus small bits of veggies. Spot-clean daily and fully clean weekly. They're nocturnal, so let them sleep during the day."],
  ["how do i take care of a rabbit | bunny care | pet rabbit tips", "Rabbits need unlimited hay, fresh water, leafy greens and a few pellets each day. Give them space to hop, chew toys and a litter box. Handle gently — support their back and hind legs — and visit a vet who knows rabbits."],
  ["how do i take care of a guinea pig | guinea pig care", "Guinea pigs need a spacious cage, unlimited hay, fresh veggies daily and vitamin C (from food like bell peppers). They're social, so they're happiest with a guinea pig buddy. Clean the cage regularly and give them hideouts."],
  ["what pet should i get | best pet for kids | good first pet", "It depends on your time and space! Fish are low-maintenance, hamsters and guinea pigs are small and gentle, cats are fairly independent, and dogs need the most time but love lots of activity. Talk with your family about who will feed, clean and care for it."],
  ["how often should i walk my dog | how much exercise does a dog need", "Most dogs need at least one or two walks a day, plus play time — anywhere from 30 minutes to 2 hours of activity depending on breed, age and energy level. A tired dog is usually a happy, well-behaved dog."],
  ["what foods are bad for dogs | can dogs eat chocolate | toxic food for dogs", "Don't feed dogs chocolate, grapes or raisins, onions, garlic, xylitol (a sweetener in some gum and candy), macadamia nuts or alcohol. If your dog eats something toxic, call a vet right away."],
  ["how do i give my dog a bath | bathing a dog | washing a dog", "Brush your dog first, then use lukewarm water and dog shampoo (not human shampoo). Keep water out of eyes and ears, rinse really well, and towel dry. Lots of treats and a calm voice help make it fun."],
  ["how do i start a garden | gardening for beginners | gardening basics", "Gardening basics:\n1. Pick a sunny spot (most veggies need 6–8 hours of sun).\n2. Start small — a few pots or a little bed.\n3. Use good soil or compost.\n4. Choose easy plants like tomatoes, lettuce, herbs, beans or sunflowers.\n5. Water when the top inch of soil is dry and pull weeds regularly."],
  ["how do i grow plants from seeds | planting seeds | how to plant seeds", "Fill a small pot with seed-starting soil, plant seeds about 2–3 times as deep as they are wide, and water gently. Keep the soil moist and warm. Once sprouts appear, give them plenty of light, then move them to bigger pots or outside when they're sturdy."],
  ["how often should i water my plants | watering plants | how much water do plants need", "Stick your finger about an inch into the soil — if it's dry, water deeply until it drains out the bottom. Most houseplants need water about once a week, but it depends on the plant, the pot and the season. Overwatering is a common mistake!"],
  ["how do i take care of houseplants | indoor plant care | why is my plant dying", "Houseplant tips: give the right light (check the plant's needs), water only when the soil is dry, use pots with drainage holes, wipe dust off leaves and feed with plant food in spring and summer. Yellow leaves often mean too much water; crispy brown ones can mean too little."],
  ["what plants are easy to grow | easy plants for beginners | hard to kill plants", "Easy plants: pothos, snake plants, spider plants and succulents indoors; and outdoors try sunflowers, marigolds, tomatoes, beans, radishes, lettuce, mint and basil."],
  ["how do i grow herbs | herb garden | growing basil", "Grow herbs like basil, mint, parsley and chives in pots on a sunny windowsill. Water when the soil feels dry and pinch off the top leaves often — it makes the plant bushier. Keep mint in its own pot because it spreads fast!"],
  ["how do i make compost | composting basics | what is compost", "Compost is rotted food scraps and yard waste that make great soil. Mix greens (fruit and veggie scraps, coffee grounds, grass) with browns (dry leaves, cardboard, paper), keep it slightly damp and turn it every week or two. Skip meat, dairy and oils."],
  ["how do i grow a bean in a cup | bean in a jar experiment | sprouting seeds", "Fold a damp paper towel inside a clear cup or jar and tuck a dried bean between the towel and the glass. Keep the towel moist and put it somewhere warm. In a few days you'll see roots and a sprout — a fun science project!"],
  ["what should i pack for a trip | packing list | packing for vacation", "Packing list basics:\n1. Clothes for each day plus one extra outfit, pajamas and underwear.\n2. Toiletries: toothbrush, toothpaste, hairbrush, deodorant.\n3. Any medicines and glasses.\n4. Chargers and headphones.\n5. Snacks, a water bottle and something to do on the way.\nCheck the weather where you're going!"],
  ["how do i pack a suitcase | packing tips | how to fit more in a suitcase", "Roll clothes instead of folding to save space and reduce wrinkles. Put heavy items like shoes at the bottom near the wheels, fill gaps with socks, and keep liquids in a sealed bag. Pack a change of clothes in your carry-on just in case."],
  ["what should i bring on a camping trip | camping packing list | camping essentials", "Camping essentials: tent, sleeping bag and pad, flashlight or headlamp, layers and a rain jacket, sturdy shoes, water and snacks, first aid kit, sunscreen and bug spray, and a trash bag to leave no trace. Always camp with an adult."],
  ["what should i bring to a sleepover | sleepover packing list", "Bring pajamas, a change of clothes, toothbrush and toothpaste, a pillow and sleeping bag or blanket, any medicine you need, and a fun game or movie idea to share. Don't forget to say thank you to the host family!"],
  ["what do i do on a long car ride | road trip activities | bored in the car", "Car ride ideas: play I Spy, the license plate game or 20 Questions, listen to audiobooks or podcasts, make a playlist, bring a sketchbook, and pack snacks. Take breaks every couple of hours to stretch."],
  ["how do i not get carsick | car sickness tips | motion sickness", "Sit where you can look out the front window, look at the horizon instead of a screen or book, keep fresh air flowing, and eat light snacks like crackers. Taking short breaks helps too."],
  ["how do i tie my shoes | how to tie shoelaces | tying shoes | bunny ears method", "Bunny ears method:\n1. Cross the laces and tuck one under to make a knot; pull tight.\n2. Make a loop (a bunny ear) with each lace.\n3. Cross the two loops over each other.\n4. Tuck one loop under and through the hole.\n5. Pull both loops tight. Practice makes perfect!"],
  ["how do i tie a tie | how to tie a necktie | tie knot", "Simple four-in-hand knot:\n1. Drape the tie around your collar, wide end on your right, hanging lower than the narrow end.\n2. Cross the wide end over the narrow end.\n3. Wrap it under and back across the front.\n4. Bring it up through the neck loop from underneath.\n5. Push it down through the front knot and tighten while sliding the knot up."],
  ["how do i keep my shoelaces from coming untied | shoelaces keep untying | double knot", "After tying your bow, tie the two loops together into a second knot (a double knot). Or try the extra-loop trick: wrap the loop around twice before pulling it through. Flat laces usually stay tied better than round ones."],
  ["how do i change a light bulb | replacing a light bulb | how to change a lightbulb", "To change a light bulb:\n1. Turn off the switch and let the old bulb cool.\n2. Use a sturdy step stool if needed (have an adult help).\n3. Twist the old bulb counterclockwise to remove it.\n4. Screw in a new bulb of the right type and wattage clockwise until snug.\n5. Turn the light back on to test."],
  ["what does a circuit breaker do | the power went out in one room | breaker tripped", "A circuit breaker shuts off power when a circuit is overloaded to keep wiring safe. If one area loses power, an adult can unplug a few things, find the breaker panel, and flip the tripped switch fully off and then back on. If it keeps tripping, call an electrician."],
  ["how do i unclog a toilet | toilet is clogged | how to use a plunger", "Put a plunger over the drain hole so it seals, then push down gently and pull up firmly several times. If the water is rising, turn off the valve behind the toilet. Avoid flushing again until it drains. Get an adult if it won't clear."],
  ["how do i fold a fitted sheet | folding fitted sheets", "Tuck one corner into the matching corner on each end so the sheet is folded in half, then tuck those corners together. Lay it flat, fold the curved edges in to make a rectangle, then fold it into a neat square."],
  ["how do i wrap a present | gift wrapping tips | how to wrap a gift", "Place the box upside down on wrapping paper and cut enough to go all the way around with a little overlap. Tape one edge, pull the other over and tape it. For the ends, fold the sides in, then fold the top and bottom flaps up and tape. Add a bow!"],
  ["how do i plan a birthday party | party planning | birthday party ideas", "Pick a date and theme, make a guest list, send invitations a couple of weeks early, plan simple games or activities, choose food and cake, and decorate. Have a little goodie bag or thank-you for guests and send thank-you notes afterward."],
  ["how do i be safe at home alone | home alone safety | staying home alone tips", "Home alone safety: keep doors locked, don't tell callers or people at the door that you're alone, know emergency numbers and how to reach your parents, stay away from the stove unless allowed, and follow your family's rules. Call emergency services in a real emergency."],
  ["what should i do in a fire | fire safety tips | fire escape plan", "Fire safety: get out fast and stay out — don't stop for belongings. Crawl low under smoke, feel doors with the back of your hand before opening, and meet your family at a planned outside spot. Then call emergency services. If your clothes catch fire: **stop, drop and roll**."],
  ["how do i cross the street safely | road safety | pedestrian safety", "Stop at the curb, look left, right and left again, and listen for cars. Cross at crosswalks or corners, walk (don't run), keep looking while crossing, and make eye contact with drivers. Put phones and headphones away near traffic."],
  ["how do i ride a bike safely | bike safety tips | do i need a helmet", "Always wear a helmet that fits snugly. Ride on the right side with traffic, use hand signals, wear bright clothes, use lights at night, and check your brakes and tires before riding."],
  ["how do i stay safe online | internet safety tips | online safety for kids", "Online safety: never share your address, school, passwords or photos with strangers; use strong, unique passwords; don't click strange links; and tell a trusted adult if something online makes you uncomfortable. Remember: people aren't always who they say they are."],
  ["how do i dress for cold weather | staying warm in winter | what to wear when it's cold", "Dress in layers: a base layer that wicks sweat, a warm middle layer like fleece, and a windproof, waterproof jacket. Add a hat, gloves, scarf and warm socks — and stay dry, because wet clothes make you cold fast."],
  ["how do i stay cool in hot weather | beat the heat | heat safety tips", "Drink plenty of water, wear light loose clothing and a hat, stay in shade during the hottest part of the day, and use fans or cool showers. Watch for dizziness, headache or nausea — signs to get out of the heat and rest."],
  ["how do i tell time on a clock | reading an analog clock | how to read a clock", "The short hand shows the hour and the long hand shows the minutes. Each number on the clock stands for 5 minutes for the long hand — so if it points at 3, that's 15 minutes. If the short hand is just past 4 and the long hand is on 6, it's 4:30."],
  ["how do i use a map | reading a map | how to read a map", "Find the compass rose to know which way is north, check the legend (key) to understand the symbols, and use the scale to measure distance. Turn the map so it matches the direction you're facing to make following it easier."],
  ["how do i find north without a compass | which way is north", "In the Northern Hemisphere, the sun rises in the east and sets in the west, so at midday it's roughly to the south. At night, find the North Star at the end of the Little Dipper's handle — it points north."],
  ["how do i whistle | learning to whistle | i can't whistle", "Wet your lips, pucker them into a small round O, rest your tongue just behind your bottom teeth and blow gently. Adjust your lip shape and tongue until you hear a tone. It usually takes some practice!"],
  ["how do i jump rope | jumping rope tips | skipping rope", "Hold the handles at hip height, swing the rope over your head using your wrists, and hop just an inch or two off the ground as it comes toward your feet. Start slow, land softly on the balls of your feet and keep a steady rhythm."],
  ["how do i learn to swim | swimming tips for beginners", "Take lessons with a qualified instructor. Beginners start by getting comfortable putting their face in the water, floating, kicking while holding the wall, and blowing bubbles. Always swim where an adult or lifeguard is watching."],
  ["how do i learn something new | learning a new skill | how to get good at something", "Break the skill into small parts, practice a little every day, focus on what's hard instead of what's easy, get feedback from someone better, and be patient — everyone starts as a beginner."],
  ["how do i keep a journal | journaling ideas | what to write in a diary", "Write a few lines each day about what happened, how you felt, something you're grateful for, or a goal. You can also doodle, make lists or write stories. There's no wrong way — it's just for you."],
  ["what is gratitude | how to practice gratitude | gratitude journal", "Gratitude means noticing and appreciating good things in your life. Try writing down three things you're thankful for each day, or telling someone thanks for something specific. It's a simple habit that can boost your mood."],
  ["how do i help my community | volunteer ideas for kids | ways to help others", "Ideas: pick up litter at a park, donate toys or clothes, help a neighbor carry groceries, make cards for a senior center, run a food drive, or volunteer with an adult at an animal shelter."],
  ["how do i recycle | what can be recycled | recycling tips", "Most places accept clean paper, cardboard, plastic bottles and jugs, cans and glass jars. Rinse containers, flatten boxes and check your local rules because they differ. Plastic bags, greasy pizza boxes and food usually don't belong in the bin."],
  ["how do i save energy at home | energy saving tips | how to save electricity", "Turn off lights and devices when you leave a room, unplug chargers you're not using, take shorter showers, use LED bulbs, close doors and windows when heating or cooling, and run washers and dishwashers with full loads."],
  ["how do i get rid of a bad smell in the fridge | smelly fridge", "Throw out spoiled food, wipe the shelves with warm water and a little baking soda, then leave an open box of baking soda inside to absorb odors. Change it every few months."],
  ["how do i clean a microwave | microwave cleaning hack", "Microwave a bowl of water with a few lemon slices or a splash of vinegar for about 3–5 minutes until steamy. Leave the door closed a few minutes, then carefully remove the bowl (it's hot!) and wipe the inside clean."],
  ["how do i set the table | table setting | where does the fork go", "Put the plate in the middle, fork on the left, knife on the right with the blade facing the plate, and spoon to the right of the knife. The napkin goes on the left or on the plate, and the glass goes above the knife. Tip: left has 4 letters like fork; right has 5 like knife and spoon."],
  ["how do i make a grocery list | grocery shopping tips | shopping on a budget", "Check what you already have, plan meals for the week, and write a list grouped by store section (produce, dairy, pantry). Stick to the list, compare unit prices and don't shop hungry!"],
  ["how do i pick good fruit at the store | choosing ripe fruit | picking produce", "Look for bright color and no big bruises or mold. Fruits like peaches and pears should give slightly when pressed; melons should feel heavy and smell sweet at the stem end; bananas ripen at home, so green ones are fine."],
  ["how do i take care of my shoes | cleaning sneakers | how to clean white shoes", "Knock off dirt, remove the laces, and scrub with a soft brush and warm soapy water. Wipe clean and let them air dry stuffed with paper towels — not in the dryer. Wash laces separately."]
);
